B.a0D=new A.co([1,"Chapter 1 sets the scene. The two great armies face each other at Kurukshetra. As Krishna drives Arjuna between them, Arjuna sees his own teachers, elders, and kinsmen arrayed to fight, and is overwhelmed with grief and doubt, laying down his bow. His despair is the very human crisis that the whole Gita sets out to answer.",2,"Chapter 2, Sankhya Yoga, is the philosophical heart of the Gita. Overwhelmed on the battlefield, Arjuna refuses to fight. Krishna answers his despair with the core teaching: the soul is eternal and never truly dies, so act without fear; do your duty for its own sake, not for its rewards; and grow into a person of steady wisdom \u2014 calm and free amid life\u2019s ups and downs.",3,"Chapter 3, Karma Yoga, is the yoga of action. Arjuna asks: if wisdom is higher, why act at all? Krishna answers that no one can avoid action, so the real key is to act selflessly, doing your duty without clinging to results, as an offering, for the good of the world. He closes by naming desire and anger as the enemies to master.",4,"Chapter 4 reveals the timeless source of this teaching and the mystery of the divine descents: whenever righteousness declines, the divine takes form to restore it. Krishna explains how the wise act without being bound, describes many kinds of sacrifice, and praises knowledge as the fire that burns away all karma.",5,"Chapter 5, Karma Sanyasa Yoga, reconciles two paths. Arjuna asks whether it is better to renounce action or to act; Krishna answers that both lead to freedom, but selfless action is the easier road. The truly free person acts without attachment, offering everything to the divine, and stays unstained like a lotus leaf on water.",6,"Chapter 6, Dhyana Yoga, is the Gita\u2019s guide to meditation. Krishna describes how to sit, steady the breath, and still the mind \u2014 like a lamp that does not flicker in a windless place. When Arjuna protests that the mind is as restless as the wind, Krishna reassures him: with steady practice and non-attachment, even the mind can be tamed, and no sincere effort is ever wasted.",7,"Chapter 7, Jnana Vijnana Yoga, turns to knowing the divine directly. Krishna reveals his lower nature \u2014 the elements, mind, and ego \u2014 and his higher nature, the life that sustains all, declaring that everything is strung upon him like beads on a thread: the taste in water, the light in the sun, the life in all beings. He names four kinds of seekers and calls the wise lover of God the dearest of all.",8,"Chapter 8, Akshara Brahma Yoga, is about the Imperishable and the moment of death. Arjuna asks Krishna to explain Brahman, the Self, action, and how the Lord is known at life\u2019s end. Krishna teaches that whatever we remember as we leave the body, we become \u2014 so remember Him always, and act. He describes the vast days and nights of Brahma in which worlds arise and dissolve, and the two paths of light and smoke by which souls depart.",9,"Chapter 9, Raja Vidya Raja Guhya Yoga, is the royal knowledge and the royal secret. Krishna reveals that the whole universe rests in him as the wind rests in space, yet he remains untouched. He is the father, mother, friend, and refuge of all. He asks nothing grand: a leaf, a flower, a fruit, or a little water offered with love is enough. Whoever turns to him, whatever their past or birth, is never lost.",10,"Chapter 10, Vibhuti Yoga, unveils the divine glories. Krishna declares himself the source of all, from whom every quality \u2014 wisdom, patience, fear, and fearlessness \u2014 arises. When Arjuna asks how to recognize him, Krishna names his chief manifestations: the sun among lights, Om among words, the Ganga among rivers, the Himalaya among mountains. Wherever there is greatness or beauty, it is a spark of his splendor \u2014 and a single fragment of him holds up the entire universe."],t.TM)
